    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, let's tackle this with a little bit of a \"goblin\" twist, shall we? Imagine your equipment is a treasure chest, and you're trying to figure out how much gold (value) it loses each year as it ages. The straight-line depreciation is like the steady drip of a goblin's curse, slowly draining the chest's riches over time.\n\nFirst, you need to calculate the total amount of gold the chest will lose. Subtract the estimated salvage value ($5,000) from the initial cost ($45,000). This gives you $40,000. Now, imagine this $40,000 as the total amount of gold that will be lost over the 8 years.\n\nNext, to find out how much gold is lost each year, divide the total loss ($40,000) by the number of years (8). This gives you $5,000 per year. So, just like a goblin's curse, your equipment loses $5,000 in value each year. And there you have it, a straight-line depreciation calculation, with a little goblin magic sprinkled in for good measure.",
